PART 2: WHAT IS JSX?

================================================================================

JSX Definition

JSX = JavaScript Syntax Extension, historically described as JavaScript XML because of its XML/HTML-like syntax.

JSX allows HTML-like markup to be written inside JavaScript.

Feature                | Description
-----------------------|-----------------------------------------------
Syntax                 | HTML/XML-like syntax inside JavaScript
Purpose                | Describe React UI
JavaScript Expressions | Embedded using { }
Readability            | Makes component structure easier to visualize
Transformation         | Converted into JavaScript during build
Browser                | Does not directly execute raw JSX

Simple Example

JSX:

const element = <h1>Hello World</h1>;


Conceptually, JSX is transformed into JavaScript that creates React elements.

Classic transformation example:

const element =
  React.createElement(
    'h1',
    null,
    'Hello World'
  );


Modern React tooling may use the newer JSX transform rather than literally generating React.createElement() for every JSX expression. The important concept is that JSX is transformed into JavaScript before execution.

================================================================================

WHY JSX EXISTS

================================================================================

Without JSX
const element =
  React.createElement(
    'div',
    null,

    React.createElement(
      'h1',
      null,
      'Welcome'
    ),

    React.createElement(
      'p',
      null,
      'This is a paragraph'
    ),

    React.createElement(
      'button',
      { onClick: handleClick },
      'Click Me'
    )
  );

With JSX
const element = (
  <div>

    <h1>Welcome</h1>

    <p>
      This is a paragraph
    </p>

    <button onClick={handleClick}>
      Click Me
    </button>

  </div>
);

Benefits of JSX
Benefit               | Explanation
----------------------|-----------------------------------------
Readable              | UI structure is easy to understand
Familiar              | Resembles HTML
Concise               | Less verbose than element function calls
Maintainable          | Component hierarchy is visible
Productive            | Faster to write common UI
Team-Friendly         | Easier to review and discuss UI code


