PART 3: JSX SYNTAX RULES

================================================================================

Rule 1: JSX Must Have a Single Parent / Root

================================================================================

A JSX expression being returned must form one valid JSX tree.

Wrong
export default function App() {
  return (
    <h1>Title</h1>
    <p>Paragraph</p>
  );
}


These are two adjacent top-level elements.

Correct Option 1: Wrapper Element
export default function App() {
  return (
    <div>

      <h1>Title</h1>

      <p>Paragraph</p>

    </div>
  );
}

Correct Option 2: Fragment
export default function App() {
  return (
    <>

      <h1>Title</h1>

      <p>Paragraph</p>

    </>
  );
}

div vs Fragment
Wrapper     | Result
------------|--------------------------------------------
<div>       | Creates an actual DOM element
Fragment <> | Groups elements without an extra DOM element


Easy memory:

MULTIPLE ELEMENTS
       ↓
WRAP THEM
       ↓
<div>...</div>

OR

<>...</>


================================================================================

Rule 2: Use className Instead of class

================================================================================

In React JSX, CSS classes are assigned using:

className

Wrong
<div class="container">
  <h1>Title</h1>
</div>

Correct
<div className="container">
  <h1>Title</h1>
</div>


When rendered to the browser DOM, the corresponding HTML has a class attribute.

Real-World Example
export default function Button() {
  return (
    <button className="btn btn-primary">
      Click Me
    </button>
  );
}


Easy memory:

HTML          | JSX
--------------|----------------
class         | className
for           | htmlFor


================================================================================

Rule 3: Use Curly Braces for JavaScript Expressions

================================================================================

JavaScript expressions used inside JSX are placed inside:

{ }

Wrong
const name = 'Alice';
const age = 25;

<h1>
  Hello name, you are age years old
</h1>


Output:

Hello name, you are age years old

Correct
const name = 'Alice';
const age = 25;

<h1>
  Hello {name}, you are {age} years old
</h1>


Output:

Hello Alice, you are 25 years old

Common Expressions
Variable
const title = 'Welcome';

<h1>{title}</h1>

Calculation
<p>2 + 2 = {2 + 2}</p>


Output:

2 + 2 = 4

Function Call
const getName = () => 'Alice';

<h1>Hello {getName()}</h1>

Ternary Operator
const isLoggedIn = true;

<p>
  {isLoggedIn ? 'Welcome!' : 'Please login'}
</p>

Array map()
const items = [
  'Apple',
  'Banana',
  'Orange'
];

<ul>
  {items.map(item => (
    <li key={item}>
      {item}
    </li>
  ))}
</ul>

Quick Reference
Requirement       | JSX
------------------|---------------------------------------
Variable          | {name}
Calculation       | {2 + 2}
Function Call     | {getName()}
Conditional       | {condition ? A : B}
Array Rendering   | {items.map(...)}


================================================================================

Rule 4: Self-Closing Elements Must Be Closed

================================================================================

Elements without children should use self-closing JSX syntax.

Correct
<img src="image.jpg" />

<input type="text" />

<br />

<hr />


Also commonly seen in document/head markup:

<meta charSet="UTF-8" />

<link
  rel="stylesheet"
  href="style.css"
/>

Easy Memory
NO CHILD CONTENT
      ↓
SELF-CLOSING
      ↓
<Element />


================================================================================

Rule 5: Many JSX DOM Properties Use React Naming

================================================================================

React event-handler properties use camelCase.

Wrong
<div onclick="handleClick()">
  Click
</div>

Correct
<div onClick={handleClick}>
  Click
</div>


Another example:

<input onChange={handleChange} />

Common Conversions
HTML-style Name   | JSX / React Name
------------------|-------------------
onclick           | onClick
onchange          | onChange
onsubmit          | onSubmit
onmouseover       | onMouseOver
onkeydown         | onKeyDown
onkeyup           | onKeyUp
ondblclick        | onDoubleClick
onfocus           | onFocus
onblur            | onBlur
class             | className
for               | htmlFor


Important: Attributes beginning with data- and aria- remain lowercase/hyphenated.

Example:

<div data-testid="container">
  Content
</div>


