PART 4: HOW JSX GETS TRANSFORMED

================================================================================

JSX Transformation Process

Browsers do not directly execute JSX syntax.

Simplified flow:

YOU WRITE JSX
      ↓
BUILD / TRANSFORM TOOL
      ↓
JAVASCRIPT
      ↓
REACT ELEMENT DESCRIPTION
      ↓
REACT RENDERING
      ↓
DOM OUTPUT


================================================================================

Step 1: Write JSX

================================================================================

const element = (
  <h1 className="title">
    Hello World
  </h1>
);


================================================================================

Step 2: Transform JSX to JavaScript

================================================================================

Classic JSX transformation example:

const element =
  React.createElement(
    'h1',
    {
      className: 'title'
    },
    'Hello World'
  );


Historically tools such as Babel commonly performed this transformation. Modern tooling can use the newer JSX runtime.

================================================================================

Step 3: React Element Representation

================================================================================

Conceptually, the resulting React element contains information similar to:

{
  type: 'h1',
  props: {
    className: 'title',
    children: 'Hello World'
  }
}


This representation tells React what UI is desired.

================================================================================

Step 4: React Commits the UI to the DOM

================================================================================

Browser output:

<h1 class="title">
  Hello World
</h1>


================================================================================

JSX TRANSFORMATION EXAMPLES

================================================================================

Example 1: Simple Element

JSX:

<h1>Hello</h1>


Classic conceptual transformation:

React.createElement(
  'h1',
  null,
  'Hello'
);


Conceptual React element:

{
  type: 'h1',
  props: {
    children: 'Hello'
  }
}


Browser result:

<h1>Hello</h1>


================================================================================

Example 2: Element with Properties

================================================================================

JSX:

<button
  className="btn"
  onClick={handleClick}
>
  Click Me
</button>


Classic transformation:

React.createElement(
  'button',
  {
    className: 'btn',
    onClick: handleClick
  },
  'Click Me'
);


Conceptually:

{
  type: 'button',

  props: {
    className: 'btn',
    onClick: handleClick,
    children: 'Click Me'
  }
}


The browser renders a button, while React manages the event relationship.

================================================================================

Example 3: Nested Elements

================================================================================

JSX:

<div>
  <h1>Title</h1>
  <p>Paragraph</p>
</div>


Classic transformation:

React.createElement(
  'div',
  null,

  React.createElement(
    'h1',
    null,
    'Title'
  ),

  React.createElement(
    'p',
    null,
    'Paragraph'
  )
);


Conceptually:

div
├── h1
│   └── Title
└── p
    └── Paragraph


Rendered HTML:

<div>
  <h1>Title</h1>
  <p>Paragraph</p>
</div>


================================================================================

Example 4: Using Variables

================================================================================

JSX:

const name = 'Alice';

<h1>Hello {name}</h1>


Conceptually:

name = "Alice"
      ↓
{name}
      ↓
Value inserted into JSX
      ↓
Hello Alice


Rendered output:

<h1>Hello Alice</h1>


