PART 5: HOW REACT TREATS THE DOM

================================================================================

What is the DOM?

DOM = Document Object Model

It is the browser's object/tree representation of an HTML document.

DOM Feature          | Description
---------------------|------------------------------------------
Representation       | Represents the HTML document
Structure            | Tree of nodes/elements
JavaScript Access    | JS can inspect/change elements
Browser              | Uses document structures to render UI

Example DOM Tree
Document
└── html
    ├── head
    │   ├── title
    │   └── meta
    │
    └── body
        ├── div#root
        │   ├── h1.title
        │   ├── p.description
        │   └── button.btn
        │
        └── script


================================================================================

VIRTUAL DOM / REACT'S IN-MEMORY UI REPRESENTATION

================================================================================

"Virtual DOM" is a common way of describing React's in-memory representation of the desired UI.

Aspect          | React Representation        | Browser DOM
----------------|-----------------------------|----------------------------
Location        | JavaScript memory           | Browser document
Visual          | Not directly displayed      | Rendered to user
Purpose         | Describe/reconcile UI        | Represents document nodes
Update Handling | React processes changes     | React commits required changes


Rather than treating the Virtual DOM as simply "faster than the DOM," the more useful concept is:

DEVELOPER DESCRIBES UI
        ↓
REACT RENDERS
        ↓
REACT RECONCILES
        ↓
REQUIRED DOM CHANGES
        ↓
BROWSER DISPLAYS RESULT


================================================================================

HOW REACT UPDATES THE DOM - STEP BY STEP

================================================================================

Step 1: Initial Render

Component:

export default function App() {
  return <h1>Hello World</h1>;
}


Conceptually React receives a UI description such as:

h1
└── "Hello World"


Then React commits the required DOM:

<h1>Hello World</h1>


================================================================================

Step 2: State Changes

================================================================================

Example:

setCount(count + 1);


Suppose the old rendered result is:

h1
└── "Count: 0"


After the state update, the component renders:

h1
└── "Count: 1"


================================================================================

Step 3: Reconciliation

================================================================================

React compares the previous rendered structure with the new rendered structure.

Previous
--------
Element  → h1
Text     → Count: 0


New
---
Element  → h1
Text     → Count: 1


React determines:

Element Type
→ Same

Text
→ Changed

Required Change
→ Update text


This general process is called reconciliation.

================================================================================

Step 4: Commit Required DOM Changes

================================================================================

Before:

<h1>Count: 0</h1>


After:

<h1>Count: 1</h1>


Conceptually:

STATE CHANGE
     ↓
RENDER
     ↓
RECONCILIATION
     ↓
DETERMINE REQUIRED CHANGES
     ↓
COMMIT DOM UPDATE
     ↓
UPDATED UI


This does not require a full browser page reload.

================================================================================

WHY THIS MATTERS FOR PERFORMANCE

================================================================================

React's model allows developers to describe the desired UI instead of manually coordinating every DOM mutation.

Manual DOM Approach            | React Approach
-------------------------------|----------------------------------------
Developer finds elements       | Component describes UI
Developer updates DOM          | State/props drive rendering
Developer synchronizes UI      | React performs reconciliation
DOM logic spread through code  | Rendering logic stays component-oriented


Performance depends on the application and implementation. React does not guarantee that every state update becomes exactly one DOM operation, but reconciliation helps determine the DOM work required to reflect the new UI.

