Example 4: Event Handling with onClick

================================================================================

import { useState } from 'react';

export default function Counter() {

  const [count, setCount] =
    useState(0);

  const handleIncrement = () => {
    setCount(count + 1);
  };

  const handleDecrement = () => {
    setCount(count - 1);
  };

  const handleReset = () => {
    setCount(0);
  };

  return (
    <div>

      <h1>
        Count: {count}
      </h1>

      <button onClick={handleIncrement}>
        Increment
      </button>

      <button onClick={handleDecrement}>
        Decrement
      </button>

      <button onClick={handleReset}>
        Reset
      </button>

    </div>
  );
}

Event Handler Table
Button       | Handler             | State Change
-------------|---------------------|-----------------
Increment    | handleIncrement     | count + 1
Decrement    | handleDecrement     | count - 1
Reset        | handleReset         | count = 0

User Interaction Flow
1. User clicks Increment
        ↓
2. onClick fires
        ↓
3. handleIncrement() runs
        ↓
4. setCount(count + 1)
        ↓
5. State update schedules rendering
        ↓
6. Component renders with new count
        ↓
7. React reconciles the result
        ↓
8. Required DOM update is committed
        ↓
9. Browser displays new count
        ↓
10. User sees Count: 1


