PART 8: BEST PRACTICES FOR JSX

================================================================================

Best Practice 1: Keep JSX Simple and Readable
Harder to Read
return (
  <div>

    {data.map(item => (

      <div key={item.id}>

        {item.users.map(user => (

          <div key={user.id}>
            {user.name}
          </div>

        ))}

      </div>

    ))}

  </div>
);

Better Organization
return (
  <div>

    {data.map(item => (

      <ItemComponent
        key={item.id}
        item={item}
      />

    ))}

  </div>
);


The detailed rendering logic can be moved into ItemComponent.

================================================================================

Best Practice 2: Use Fragments When an Extra DOM Element Isn't Needed

================================================================================

Unnecessary wrapper:

return (
  <div>

    <h1>Title</h1>

    <p>Content</p>

  </div>
);


If no actual wrapper is required:

return (
  <>

    <h1>Title</h1>

    <p>Content</p>

  </>
);

<div>       → Creates DOM element
Fragment    → Groups without extra DOM element


Use a real semantic/container element when the layout or HTML structure actually requires one.

================================================================================

Best Practice 3: Use Stable Keys in Lists

================================================================================

Less suitable for reorderable/dynamic lists:

{items.map((item, index) => (

  <li key={index}>
    {item}
  </li>

))}


Preferred when data provides a stable ID:

{items.map(item => (

  <li key={item.id}>
    {item.name}
  </li>

))}

Why?
Array Index              | Stable ID
-------------------------|----------------------------------
May change after reorder | Stays associated with the item
May shift after deletion | Identifies the same data item
Can confuse identity     | Better supports reconciliation


================================================================================

Best Practice 4: Use Meaningful Variable Names

================================================================================

Less clear:

const x = true;
const y = 'Alice';

return (
  <div>
    {x ? <h1>{y}</h1> : null}
  </div>
);


Clearer:

const isLoggedIn = true;
const userName = 'Alice';

return (
  <div>
    {isLoggedIn
      ? <h1>{userName}</h1>
      : null}
  </div>
);

Unclear       | Better
--------------|----------------
x             | isLoggedIn
y             | userName


